course: money
lesson: 21
title: Bills: rent, mortgage, energy, water, phone, council tax
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A bill is a letter or message asking a family to pay for something it uses: electricity and gas for light and heat, water from the tap, the phone, and the home itself.
explain: Picture Sophie's fridge with a list stuck on it: rent £650, energy £120, water £40, phone £30, council tax £110; add them up and £950 has to go out every month before anyone thinks about treats.
explain: Rent is paid to a landlord who owns the home; a mortgage is a big loan from a bank to buy a home, paid back over many years with extra called interest; both are normal ways to have a roof over your head.
explain: Council tax goes to the local council for shared things like bins, streetlights and libraries, so the bin men are paid even though nobody hands them money at the door.
explain: Small habits add up: a light left on all day costs about 10p, which is £3 in a month, and paying a bill late can add a fee on top.
fact: A bill is a request to pay for something you have used, such as electricity, gas, water or a phone.
fact: Energy means electricity and gas; when its price goes up, the same use costs more, so a bill can go from £80 to £100, which is £20 more.
fact: Rent is money paid every month to live in a home that belongs to someone else, the landlord; at £700 a month it comes to 12 × £700 = £8,400 a year.
fact: Renting a home is very normal; millions of families rent.
fact: A mortgage is a loan from a bank to buy a home, paid back every month for many years, often 25; because of interest, the total paid back is more than the house cost.
fact: Council tax is paid to the local council for local services such as bins, streetlights, roads and libraries; £110 a month is 12 × £110 = £1,320 a year.
fact: Fixed costs are bills that come every month, like rent, energy and council tax; they are paid before money goes on fun.
fact: A phone contract is an agreement to pay for a set time: £25 a month for 24 months is 24 × £25 = £600 in total.
fact: A running tap can waste about 6 litres of water a minute; homes with a water meter pay for every litre they use.
fact: Heating costs money for every hour it is on; at about 50p an hour, one hour a day for 30 days is £15, and five hours a day is £75.
fact: Paying a bill late can add a late fee: a £60 bill with a £15 fee becomes £75.
fact: A direct debit is an automatic payment from a bank account that pays a bill on time each month, so no late fees.
exercise: Josh asks what a bill is. What is the best answer? | A request to pay for something you have used, like electricity | A present the postman brings to every house each month | A letter telling you how much money you have saved | A ticket that lets you into the cinema for free
exercise: Mum's electric bill was £80 and now it is £100. How much more is it? | £20 | £18 | £180 | £28
exercise: Dad says, "Turn the lights off, I'm not made of money!" Why does it help? | Every light uses electricity, which costs money | Lights only cost money when the telly is switched on too | Switching off makes the bulbs last for ever and ever | It doesn't help, because lights are free after dark
exercise: A light left on all day costs about 10p. How much is that over 30 days? | £3 | 30p | £30 | £1
exercise: Lily's family rents their house. Who owns it? | The landlord, who is paid rent every month | Lily's family, because they are the ones who live there | The council, because it collects the bins every week | Nobody, because rented houses have no owner
exercise: Rent is £700 a month. How much is that in a year? | £8,400 | £7,000 | £840 | £8,000
exercise: Callum's family has a mortgage. What is it? | A bank loan to buy a home, paid back over years | Money paid every month to a landlord who owns the home | A tax paid to the council for collecting the bins | A bill for all the gas a family uses during the winter
exercise: Why does a family with a mortgage pay back more than the house cost? | The bank charges interest for lending the money | Houses get dearer every single day they are lived in | The bank adds an extra fee every time it rains hard | They don't; they pay back exactly the house price
exercise: Sophie's fridge list says rent £650, energy £120, water £40, phone £30, council tax £110. What is the total? | £950 | £850 | £900 | £1,050
exercise: Ruby leaves the tap running for 2 minutes, twice a day. About 6 litres run out each minute. How many litres is that a day? | 24 litres | 12 litres | 6 litres | 48 litres
exercise: Zak asks who pays the bin men. What is the answer? | Everyone in the area, through council tax | The bin men, out of what they find in bins | The supermarket, out of the money from its sweet sales | Nobody, because collecting the bins is unpaid work
exercise: Council tax is £110 a month. How much is that in a year? | £1,320 | £1,100 | £1,210 | £132
exercise: Mum's phone contract is £25 a month for 24 months. How much in total? | £600 | £250 | £480 | £625
exercise: Gran has the heating on for 1 hour a day. It costs 50p an hour. How much for 30 days? | £15 | £5 | £150 | £1.50
exercise: A £60 bill is paid late and a £15 late fee is added. How much must Dad pay now? | £75 | £60 | £45 | £65
exercise: What is a direct debit? | An automatic payment from your bank that pays a bill on time | A red letter that arrives when a bill is late | A discount for families who use less water | A card from the bank that lets you borrow money to pay for bills
test: Your mate goes, "Rent's a scam, if you live in a house it's yours, innit." What's true? | Renting means paying the owner to live in their home, and it's very normal | He's right, living somewhere for a year makes it yours | Only people without a job ever pay any rent | Rent is money paid to the council for the bins
test: Gran from Birmingham says, "Heating's on for an hour, that's it, bab." ("Bab" means "dear".) What if she had it on 5 hours a day for 30 days, at 50p an hour? | It would cost £75 instead of £15 | It would cost £25 instead of £15 | It would cost £15 either way | It would cost £150 instead of £15
test: Your cousin says, "£25 a month for a phone is lowkey nothing, basically free." ("Lowkey" means "kind of".) On a 24-month contract, what does it really cost? | £600 in total | £25 in total | £250 in total | £300 in total
test: A dad from Glasgow says, "The leccy's gone up again, it's pure dreich oot, and we cannae have the heating on aw day." ("Leccy" is electricity; "dreich" means grey and wet.) What is he worried about? | The energy bill rising if the heating stays on all day | Rain coming through the roof into the house | The electricity man taking the telly away | The landlord putting up the rent because of all the rain
test: What would happen if a family just stopped paying council tax? | They'd get reminders and fines, and the law can make them pay | Only their own bin would be skipped, nothing else | Nothing at all, because paying council tax is optional for families | The council would send them a refund instead
test: In a cartoon, a family burns banknotes in the fire and says, "Heating's free anyway!" What is wrong? | Heating costs money, paid on the energy bill | Nothing, because heating really is free in winter | Heating is only paid for during the summer months | Heating is paid for out of the council tax instead
test: Dad, sarcastic: "Late fee, £15. Brilliant." Is he pleased, and what does his £60 bill become? | No, he's annoyed, and the bill becomes £75 | Yes, he's pleased, and the bill becomes £45 | No, he's annoyed, and the bill stays £60 | Yes, he's pleased, and the bill becomes £75
test: Your big sister says, "A mortgage is just posh rent, same thing." What is the best answer? | A mortgage is a loan to buy the home; rent pays an owner to live there | She's right, both are paid to a landlord for ever | A mortgage is a tax for living in a big house | Rent is a loan; a mortgage is a present from the bank
test: A Liverpool lad says, "Me ma says turn the tap off, la, it's like flushing quids away." ("La" means "mate"; "quids" are pounds.) Is his mum right? | Yes, water costs money and a running tap wastes about 6 litres a minute | No, tap water is completely free everywhere | No, water only costs money when it is hot | Yes, but only at night, when water is dearer
test: A girl from Dublin says, "We never get bills, me da just pays them, so they're grand." ("Grand" means fine; "da" is dad.) What's really true? | The bills still come, and her dad pays them, maybe by direct debit | She's right, some houses never have any bills | Bills only come to houses that own a car | Bills are paid out of the children's pocket money
test: Sophie's mum says the £950 of bills come before the cinema. Why? | Bills like rent and energy keep the home going, so they come first | Cinemas won't take money until your bills are paid | The bank stops people watching films in winter | Bills are smaller than fun, so they hardly matter
test: Mum says, "Rent's £700 a month — no wait, it went up, it's £750." How much is that a year? | £9,000 | £8,400 | £8,750 | £7,500
exam: Ascertain the aggregate monthly expenditure of a household whose obligations are as follows: rent £650, energy £120, water £40, telephone £30 and council tax £110. | £950 | £850 | £910 | £1,050
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a mortgage? | A loan advanced by a bank for the purchase of a dwelling, repayable with interest over many years | A sum payable monthly to a landlord in respect of the occupation of a dwelling | A charge levied by a local authority in respect of the collection of refuse | A sum paid by a bank to a householder in respect of savings deposited
exam: It is given that a telephone contract obliges the subscriber to remit twenty-five pounds per month for a period of twenty-four months. Determine the total sum payable. | £600 | £500 | £625 | £480
exam: Identify the principal purpose for which council tax is levied. | The funding of local services such as refuse collection, street lighting and libraries | The repayment of a loan taken out for the purchase of a dwelling | The remuneration of a landlord for the occupation of a property | The settlement of charges for electricity and gas consumed
